<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clifford Dupont</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1905">1905</a>–<a href="/years/1978">1978</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Clifford Dupont was a British-born Rhodesian politician and a minister in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ian-smith">Ian Smith</a>'s government at the Unilateral Declaration of Independence in 1965. Smith appointed him Officer Administering the Government in place of the British governor, and when Rhodesia declared itself a republic in 1970 he became its first president, serving until 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clifford-dupont.jpg" alt="Clifford Dupont" width="231" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Associated Press · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rhodesian_president_Clifford_Dupont_at_swearing_in_ceremony_1970_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dupont, of Huguenot ancestry, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to Alfred Walter and Winifred Mary Dupont; his father's firm dealt largely in the rag trade. He was educated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a>'s Stortford College and read law at Clare College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, qualified as a solicitor in 1929 and set up his own firm in 1933. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he served in the Royal Artillery as adjutant of a light anti-aircraft battalion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, was on General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a>'s staff during the liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1944, and ended the war at the War Office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After a visit to Southern Rhodesia in 1947 he bought land at Featherstone, south of Salisbury, and turned it into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> ranch, emigrating for good in the early 1950s. His second wife died in 1957, and in 1958 his son and daughter were killed in an air crash. That year he won the Fort Victoria federal seat for the Dominion Party, and four years later he took Charter for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodesian-front">Rhodesian Front</a> and became Minister for Justice.</p><p>With Desmond Lardner-Burke he worked to replace Prime Minister Winston Field with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ian-smith">Ian Smith</a>. In October 1964 he resigned his seat to fight Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roy-welensky">Roy Welensky</a> at a by-election in Arundel and beat him by 1079 votes to 633. As Smith's deputy he held External Affairs and, from June 1965, Defence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Dupont was the second to sign the Unilateral Declaration of Independence on 11 November 1965. Smith's government still professed loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> as Queen of Rhodesia but no longer recognised the governor, Sir Humphrey Gibbs. On 2 December Smith asked the Queen to appoint Dupont governor-general; Buckingham Palace replied that she could not entertain such advice, and the United Kingdom held Gibbs to be the only lawful authority. Opponents of UDI such as Ahrn Palley refused to recognise Dupont's office.</p><p>After a predominantly white electorate approved a republican constitution in a referendum on 24 June 1969, Dupont signed the proclamation of the republic on 2 March 1970 and became interim president. Parliament elected him president on 14 April. The office was largely ceremonial, real power remaining with Smith and his cabinet. He suffered long bouts of ill health, and he died on 28 June 1978 while undergoing radium treatment for what was believed to be cancer.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Clifford Dupont” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harare">Harare</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1952" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Clifford Dupont"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1952">What was here in 1952? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
